    "id": "10.21203/rs.3.rs-10375648/v2",
    "title": "Multi-Stage Alignment of Large Language Models for Popularity Bias Mitigation in Generative Movie Recommendation",
    "abstract": "Abstract  Large language models (LLMs) exhibit a strong popularity bias in recommendation tasks, often over-recommending mainstream items while under-representing user-aligned but less popular content. To address this limitation, we propose a multi-stage alignment pipeline for debiasing LLM-based movie recommenders, consisting of preference extraction from user histories, supervised fine-tuning (SFT) for behavioral grounding, and Direct Preference Optimization (DPO) for bias-corrective alignment toward less-popular yet topically relevant items. By explicitly separating grounding from bias correction, the framework enables stable and controllable improvements in exposure fairness. In addition to intrinsic alignment, we systematically compare our approach against classical post-hoc debiasing methods, including popularity-aware re-ranking, to examine trade-offs between recommendation quality and exposure balance. Experiments on MovieLens, Netflix, and Flickscore demonstrate that multi-stage alignment achieves substantial gains in novelty and catalog coverage while maintaining competitive accuracy and offering advantages over simpler exposure-adjustment baselines. These results position multi-stage LLM alignment as a principled and scalable strategy for mitigating popularity bias in generative recommender systems. Our code is publicly available at https://github.com/krishnakantx/debiased-llm-recsys.",
