    "id": "10.21203/rs.3.rs-11147390/v1",
    "title": "Monte Carlo simulation and breast cancer transcriptomic benchmarking characterize reliability limits of high-dimensional biomedical feature selection",
    "abstract": "Abstract  Reliable feature selection requires adequate signal recovery, few false selections and reproducible feature sets. We evaluated these requirements jointly using Monte Carlo simulation and breast cancer transcriptomic benchmarking. Breast cancer expression matrices supplied empirical covariates; signal supports and binary outcomes were artificially generated. Joint success required true-positive rates ≥ 0.80 and false-discovery proportions ≤ 0.10 in both members of a data pair, with Jaccard similarity ≥ 0.75. Broad screening covered 624 configurations and nine methods, followed by independent confirmation. The principal transcriptomic benchmark compared composite minimax concave penalty (cMCP) and fixed-mixing-parameter Elastic Net across 2,000 and 22,092 genes. With low-correlation supports and 1,000 patients per side, cMCP achieved 296 to 299 successes in 300 repetitions; high-correlation supports yielded none. Conservative tuning improved reliability when recall remained sufficient, but reduced success from 93/300 to 29/300 in a 2,000-gene, 500-patient setting. Weaker and heterogeneous effects further restricted recovery. These findings characterize conditional reliability limits rather than a universal method ranking. Joint evaluation and component-level diagnostics identify when sample size, support correlation, signal distribution and regularization permit reproducible recovery of known features.",
