    "id": "10.64898/2026.10.26.761858",
    "title": "Dynamic merging of enhancer- and promoter-associated assemblies is coupled to transcriptional bursting",
    "abstract": "Classical models attribute distal enhancer function to chromatin looping, whereas emerging models propose that spatially organized assemblies of transcriptional regulators also facilitate enhancer-promoter communication. To investigate how these processes relate to transcription, we developed an endogenous-tagging-based four-color live-cell imaging platform and an integrated quantitative analysis pipeline in mouse embryonic stem cells (mESCs), enabling simultaneous visualization of regulatory assembly dynamics, chromatin organization and transcriptional bursting at the endogenous  Sox2 locus. We find that the pluripotency transcription factor OCT4 and the coactivator BRD4 form numerous spatially distinct but dynamically interacting assemblies. At non-bursting  Sox2 alleles, the promoter and the distal  Sox2 enhancer SCR occupy distinct assembly environments, with stronger BRD4 association at the SCR than at the promoter. At bursting  Sox2 alleles, transcription is coupled to transient merging and partial overlap of enhancer- and promoter-associated assemblies, with the SCR, promoter and nascent RNA frequently sharing OCT4/BRD4-enriched assembly environments across a broad range of SCR-promoter distances. Pharmacological perturbation of bromodomain-dependent BRD4 function preferentially suppresses long-duration, high-amplitude bursts. Together, our findings support a model in which transcriptional bursting is coupled to the dynamic reorganization and merging of enhancer- and promoter-associated regulatory assemblies, linking chromatin-looping and assembly-based views of enhancer-promoter communication.",
    "journal": "",
    "doi": "10.64898/2026.10.26.761858",
