    "id": "10.1093/ismejo/wrag291",
    "title": "Design and application of a salt–alkali-resistant root synthetic microbial community for Medicago sativa L. using machine learning",
    "abstract": "AbstractSoil salinization poses a serious threat to global agricultural sustainability, underscoring the urgent need for integrated plant–microbe strategies to enable the productive utilization of saline-alkali soils. This study established a four-stage framework, comprising data-driven strain selection, functional screening, synthetic community optimization, and experimental validation, to construct root-associated synthetic microbial communities (SynComs) that enhance salt–alkali tolerance in alfalfa (Medicago sativa L.). Using 16S rRNA gene amplicon sequencing and high-throughput cultivation, we established a curated repository of 327 salt-alkali-tolerant root-associated bacterial strains. Subsequent integrative analyses, including genomic characterization, functional profiling, and pairwise interaction assays, enabled the rational selection of 20 phylogenetically and functionally complementary strains for the stochastic assembly of SynComs. Seed germination assays were conducted using 134 stochastically assembled SynComs. To decipher strain-level interaction networks, we employed two complementary machine learning approaches: Random forest (RF) and elastic-net-regularized generalized linear models (GLMNet). These models enabled the precise selection of five key functional strains for assembling the target SynCom, SP3. In pot experiments under salt–alkali stress, SP3 consistently outperformed single-strain (S1) and two-strain (D1) inoculations, increasing alfalfa plant height and root length by 62.0% and 91.1%, respectively. In field experiments in saline–alkali soil, SP3 significantly increased alfalfa aboveground dry biomass yield by 48.1% relative to the untreated control. This study provides a reproducible framework for the targeted design of root-associated SynComs, offering a strategy for harnessing plant–microbe interactions to enhance crop productivity and enable the sustainable reclamation of saline–alkali soils.",
