    "id": "10.3389/fmars.2026.2006926",
    "title": "Marine ecological responses to thermal discharge from nuclear and coal-fired power plants: a review focusing on seawater quality, plankton, and microorganisms",
    "abstract": "With the rapid expansion of coastal nuclear and coal-fired power plants, the environmental impacts of thermal discharge on adjacent marine ecosystems have become a major concern in environmental science. Unlike previous reviews focusing on a single biological group or isolated water-quality parameter, this review integrates physicochemical and biological responses, microbial functional ecology and the microbial loop, identifies candidate indicator taxa and functional traits, compares nuclear and coal-fired effluents, and synthesizes effects on seawater physicochemical properties, plankton community structure, and microbial diversity and function. We distinguish two impact pathways, which are direct entrainment/impingement within once-through cooling systems and in situ warming experienced by organisms in the discharged plume. Chlorination by-products, nutrient enrichment and discharge-modified hydrodynamics are considered because they confound field-derived signals; thus many field patterns are mixed-stressor responses and cannot be attributed to warming alone. Nuclear and coal-fired plants differ in effluent composition, in that nuclear once-through discharges during normal operation are dominated by heated seawater and biocide residues. Many field-observed patterns represent mixed-stressor responses and cannot be attributed to warming alone. Thermal discharge reduces dissolved oxygen and pH, accelerates nutrient cycling, increases pollutant bioavailability, and alters phytoplankton, zooplankton, and microbial communities, thereby affecting food-web stability and biogeochemical processes. The review also discusses current controversies and knowledge gaps. The main contribution of this review is to integrate seawater chemistry, plankton dynamics and microbial functional ecology within a single attribution framework, to identify candidate indicator taxa and functional traits. This synthesis provides a scientific basis for environmental monitoring, ecological risk assessment and adaptive management of thermal discharge in coastal marine ecosystems.",
