    "id": "10.3389/fmicb.2026.1949464",
    "title": "Mapping the knowledge landscape of dietary fiber and gut microbiota in children and adolescents: a bibliometric analysis with cross-database concordance assessment",
    "abstract": "ObjectiveThis study aimed to map the research landscape and thematic evolution of dietary fiber and gut microbiota in children and adolescents and to assess cross-database concordance of selected temporal and leading author-keyword indicators.MethodsEnglish Articles and Reviews published from 1995 to 2026 were retrieved from the Web of Science Core Collection (WoSCC), and 701 publications were included in the primary science-mapping dataset. The 2026 output was treated as an incomplete publication year. PubMed and Scopus were analyzed independently as comparison datasets. Temporal concordance over the complete years 1995–2025 was assessed using Pearson correlations of raw annual counts, first differences, and linearly detrended residuals. Thematic concordance was evaluated across Top 10, Top 20, and Top 50 author-keyword lists using overlap rates, Jaccard indices, and conditional Spearman rank correlations, with sensitivity analyses excluding predefined search-defining terms and using union lists.ResultsPublication output increased markedly after 2020. Co-citation analysis identified major knowledge-base themes including prebiotics, in vitro gut fermentation, short-chain fatty acids (SCFAs), human milk, immunity, and metabolic-health contexts, while keyword, burst, and Trend Topics analyses indicated a broadening from fermentable substrates, prebiotic-related themes, and microbiota characterization toward fermentation, microbial metabolites, and increasingly diverse health-related contexts. Raw annual publication counts were strongly correlated across databases (r = 0.952–0.986), with positive correlations remaining after first differencing (r = 0.539–0.855) and linear detrending (r = 0.831–0.952). Across Top 10, Top 20, and Top 50 author-keyword comparisons, overlap ranged from 78.0% to 95.0%, Jaccard indices from 0.639 to 0.905, and conditional Spearman coefficients from 0.743 to 0.950. Concordance remained positive in sensitivity analyses excluding search-defining terms and using union lists, although some estimates were attenuated.ConclusionThis study maps the evolving pediatric knowledge landscape of dietary fiber and gut microbiota and shows cross-database concordance for selected temporal and leading author-keyword indicators. The field increasingly emphasizes microbial function and metabolites within broader health-related contexts, supporting future research that integrates developmental stage, fiber type, food matrix, gut microbiota profiling, and microbial metabolite measurement.",
