    "id": "10.21203/rs.3.rs-11374994/v1",
    "title": "A hierarchical general video matting framework towards temporal coherence",
    "abstract": "Abstract  Video Matting is the process of accurately extracting foreground objects from a sequence of input video frames, which requires the accurate estimation not only on the transparency of foreground objects for each frame but also on the spatial-temporal object coherence between consecutive frames. Meanwhile, the prevailing data-driven deep-learning based video matting approaches are constrained by fixed datasets and limited feature aggregation and filtering patterns, making it hard to guarantee the matting results in terms of coherence and accuracy, especially of general foreground objects. Without relying on data-driven patterns, this paper proposes a novel non-data-driven based video matting approach (NDVM) towards general foreground targets and a hierarchical framework towards temporal coherence. Firstly, an KNN-based pre-process layer is employed for adjacent frames according to different foreground and background overlap patterns to classify certain number of opaque pixels. Next, an optical-flow-based sampling layer is adopted for consecutive frames combined with a re-identification criterion for opaque pixels to record both of the transparency and foreground color priors. Finally, an affinity-based smoothing layer based on adaptive kernels is utilized for inter-frames to determine the final video matting results. Experimental results demonstrate that, in terms of quantitative comparison, our approach outperforms both various previous video matting methods and the ablation intermediates, in terms of intra-frame accuracy and inter-frame temporal coherence. Qualitatively, our method also exhibits superior visual perception compared with other state-of-the-art methods. Furthermore, the final foreground results recorded in this paper also provide feasibility and quality assurance for the final video synthesis stage.",
