    "id": "10.1093/bioinformatics/btag783",
    "title": "ARCHER-LD: rapid long-range linkage disequilibrium calculations at biobank scale using GPU acceleration.",
    "abstract": "SummaryLinkage disequilibrium (LD) information from one's own dataset is considered optimal for downstream analyses such as statistical fine-mapping. However, the computational complexity (≈N22 computations for N variants) leads most studies to use external reference panels, such as from 1000 Genomes. To capture LD across all variant pairs in biobank-scale whole-genome sequencing (WGS) datasets with hundreds of millions of variants, new computational strategies are essential. We present a novel approach that uses multi-GPU distributed computing to compute R2 for every variant pair in a dataset. On chromosome 22 (1.8 million variants) of the 30× WGS 1000 Genomes dataset, our method using eight consumer-level 12 GB GPUs (NVIDIA RTX 2080TIs) takes 80 min, corresponding to a ≈8× speedup. We further demonstrate true biobank-scale performance in the Penn Medicine Biobank (57 170 samples), computing chromosome 1 LD (≈1.38 million variants) in 49 min versus 22.7 h for PLINK, a ≈28× speedup. With this method, we successfully computed on the aforementioned 30× WGS 1000 Genomes dataset (≈120 million variants and ≈2500 samples) the entire LD matrix (>1e16, or 10 quadrillion elements) in under 6 h using 512 NVIDIA 40GB A100 GPUs on the Department of Energy Argonne Leadership Computing Facility Polaris Supercomputer. We make this tool, Accelerated Rapid Computation of High-throughput Extensive Recombination and Linkage Disequilibrium (ARCHER-LD), coded in Python using CuPy, publicly available. Using this tool, researchers can leverage the full extent of their genomic data without relying on external LD reference panels and acquire more accurate, population-specific findings, particularly for groups underrepresented in existing databases.Availability and implementationARCHER-LD is an open-source tool available on GitHub under the Apache License 2.0 at https://github.com/rachitk/ARCHER-LD. The version of record used in this manuscript is archived via Zenodo at https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.23399887.",
